# About This Project
#
# This project is an ongoing Litex formalization inspired by Andre Weil and
# Maxwell Rosenlicht's Number Theory for Beginners. It follows the
# non-exercise mathematical development of the book through its thirteen
# sections, from divisibility and prime factorization to congruences, groups,
# polynomials, quadratic reciprocity, and Gaussian integers. It is an
# independent translation project, not an official edition of or substitute
# for the original book.
#
# Most declarations included here are accepted by the Litex verifier. The
# remaining unchecked arguments are kept visible as explicit trust boundaries,
# with no attempt to present them as completed proofs. Some concern steps that
# concise textbook exposition leaves implicit; others expose substantial work
# still needed in finite counting, polynomial Euclidean theory, finite fields,
# quadratic reciprocity, and Gaussian factorization. We fill in omitted
# reasoning where doing so clarifies the mathematical structure, while
# occasionally retaining a marked boundary to respect the source's pace and
# avoid overwhelming the main argument with supporting infrastructure.
#
# The later sections on residue classes, finite groups, polynomial arithmetic,
# quadratic residues, and Gaussian integers are particularly rich examples of
# how Litex connects concrete calculations with reusable abstract facts.
#
# We hope readers gain insight into elementary number theory and see both the
# promise and the current limits of expressing a long mathematical narrative
# as readable, checkable Litex code.
